The existing draft in the workspace is not defensible as written: it calls a 45-million-cell expansion the reference algorithm even though the rendered run-length data already includes rank annotations, so the paper’s reconstruction rule solves the actual encoded input in roughly the same few hundred operations as the claimed shortcut. I’m replacing that artificial gap with a family built directly from Equation (2): recover the sparse root-level enumerator from a run-length encoded square-level enumerator. Dense recurrence is the honest linear-time algorithm; the compact route is the finite-difference identity \((1-x)S=(1-x^L)Q\), and verification is an exact sparse polynomial identity.
