# Sparse root enumerators for ribbon tilings (arXiv:2408.09272)

> **Status: locally verified, but not shippable yet.** The required OpenRouter
> run was attempted on 5 September 2026. All four permitted redraws returned
> HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before an oracle answered. API
> errors are not model failures, so STEP 4 and G9(b) remain unproved.

## Profile

| field | value |
|---|---|
| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
| native domain | combinatorics |
| object regime | rational exact |
| computational core | polynomial identity |
| certificate | sparse polynomial |
| intended intuition | change of variables: take a first difference |
| domain essentiality | native |
| reduction | none |

## The problem and why the certificate is trustworthy

The source is Blackburn, Chen, and Kargin,
[*An upper bound on the per-tile entropy of ribbon tilings*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.09272).
An `L`-ribbon contains one lattice square at each of `L` consecutive diagonal
levels (Definition 1). If `sigma_l` counts region squares on level `l` and
`tau_j` counts ribbons rooted on level `j`, Equation (2) says

`sigma_l = tau_l + tau_(l-1) + ... + tau_(l-L+1)`.

The instance gives the nonzero `sigma_l` as exact constant runs and asks for the
sparse Laurent polynomial `Q(x)=sum tau_j x^j`. The generator samples `Q`
first and constructs `S=(1+x+...+x^(L-1))Q`; this is inverse generation by a
composed identity, not a solved instance. Every sampled histogram is genuinely
realizable: put the requested number of widely separated all-east ribbons at
each root level. The checker multiplies a candidate by the ribbon factor using
integer endpoint events and compares every coefficient exactly. It never reads
the planted answer, and polynomial uniqueness means any accepted witness is the
same `Q`.

## Why this is Track B

This paper does not license a Track-A hardness claim. Section 1 cites
Sheffield's algorithm, linear in region area, for simply connected ribbon
regions, and says the existence complexity for general ribbon regions is open.
The NP-completeness result mentioned there concerns 180-trominoes, not this
problem.

The disclosed reference algorithm is the direct dense recurrence derived from
Equation (2),
`tau_l = sigma_l - sigma_(l-1) + tau_(l-L)`. It is `O(D)` for a level span of
`D` and, on the recorded hard seed, performed **21,196,647 exact arithmetic
operations in 1.39 s**. Across eight hard seeds it performed at least
20,794,476 operations and averaged 1.40 s, solving 8/8 as expected. A solver
who sees the change of variables uses
`(1-x)S=(1-x^L)Q`: constant runs collapse to endpoints and sparse division
takes **160** exact additions on the recorded instance. That 21-million versus
160 gap—not computational intractability—is the Track-B claim.

## Worked demo (`seed=3`)

The complete instance data are:

```text
L = 5
terms(Q) = 3
1 <= tau_j <= 3
root exponent range = [9,19]
level runs [first,last,sigma] = [[9,13,3],[14,18,1],[19,23,2]]
```

The answer, in the renderer's exact rational-polynomial format, is:

```json
[[[3,1],[9]],[[1,1],[14]],[[2,1],[19]]]
```

`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`. Changing the final coefficient
from 2 to 3 returns `(False, "polynomial identity does not match the level
counts")`. A person can solve the demo on paper: the three five-level blocks
are visible directly. Brute force finds one valid answer among all 4,455
bounded-language candidates.

## Difficulty presets

| preset | ribbon length `L` | answer terms | chains | status |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| demo | 5 | 3 | 1 | hand-solvable illustration |
| easy | 80,003 | 32 | 8 | oracle call blocked before answer |
| medium | 400,009 | 40 | 10 | not reached |
| hard | 1,500,007 | 48 | 12 | candidate shipping preset |

`escalate()` multiplies `L` by four while keeping the 48-term certificate
fixed, so it grows the coefficient haystack rather than the answer.

## Gate results

| gate | result | measured evidence |
|---|---|---|
| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted identities verified; 12/12 JSON round trips |
| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
| G3 | pass | prose, fenced/tagged JSON, and raw JSON round-trip |
| G4 | pass | 0/200,000; exact log10 density `-308.4940` under the structure-aware prior |
| G5 | pass | one exact solution; 21,196,647 dense operations / 160 compact operations |
| G6 | pass locally | five attacks each 0/8; reference recurrence 8/8 |
| G7 | pass | doubled `L=3,000,014` built and verified |
| G8 | pass | 80/80 invariant keys, 80/80 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
| G9(c) | pass | 865 chars, about 217 tokens, 144 atoms, 160 operations |
| G9(b) | **unproved** | no oracle attempt completed because of the OpenRouter account limit |

The G6 attacks were positive finite differences, largest boundary jumps, raw
run starts, naïve endpoint pairing, and 256 random bounded sparse polynomials.
The four deterministic attacks produced four distinct candidates on every one
of the eight measured seeds.

## Oracle loop and G9 arms

The script-owned `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` contains four HTTP-error records,
all at `easy`; it is infrastructure evidence, not hardness evidence. The
redraws happened to select Grok 4.6 each time because no model completed a slot.

| arm | solved / completed attempts | conclusion |
|---|---:|---|
| bare | 0 / 0 | unavailable; all calls were API errors |
| structural hint | 0 / 0 | not run without a bare shipping verdict |
| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | not run without a bare shipping verdict |

Hinted-minus-placebo is therefore undefined. No `g9_*_transcript.jsonl` was
fabricated. When the key limit is restored, rerun the bare ladder, set
`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` to the held rung, rerun `selftest()`, and then run the two
G9 arms in separate scratch directories. The structural hint names only the
finite-difference invariant; it does not give the division procedure.

## Use

```python
from gen_2408_09272 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify

inst = make_instance(seed=3, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
print(render(inst))
candidate = parse_answer('<answer>[[[3,1],[9]],[[1,1],[14]],[[2,1],[19]]]</answer>')
assert verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
```

After successful oracle and G9 runs, emit from the repository root with:

```bash
scripts/emit.sh 2408.09272 20 hard
```

Do not emit the current provisional result as a shipped artifact.

## Caveats

- This is no-tool compression, not a claim of computational hardness. Sparse
  polynomial division solves every instance quickly once the identity is seen.
- The `P(guess)` prior already enforces the exact term count, sorted distinct
  support, inferred root range, and coefficient bound. Its tiny value measures
  blind guessing only; it says nothing about a solver that finds the first-
  difference invariant.
- The input is a level histogram, not explicit square coordinates. This is
  native to Equation (2), but it tests the paper's enumerator identity rather
  than geometric placement of individual ribbons.
- SAT/SMT encodings, computer-algebra sparse division, and learned pattern
  detectors were not tested. The successful dense recurrence is reported
  separately because Track B expects the standard algorithm to work.
- The local panel and operation gap do not replace the missing four-vendor and
  hinted-oracle evidence. Until those calls complete, hardness is not established.

